# Response headers for the static app (the SPA shell, its assets, and the
# service worker), applied by Cloudflare's static assets.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/
#
# Not applied to what the Worker answers (/api/*, `run_worker_first` in
# apps/api/wrangler.toml): those responses are JSON or redirects, never a page.
# A WebDAV proxy, if ever built (deferred, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §5.4), would set its
# own headers.
#
# The policy (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §9), checked by tests/csp.test.ts:
# - script-src 'self' only. zod would probe for `new Function` at startup and
#   report a violation even though it catches the refusal, so src/jitless.ts
#   turns the probe off before any schema is built.
# - style-src 'unsafe-inline': the editors set element styles and CodeMirror
#   inserts <style> elements (style-mod). Neither can run script.
# - img-src https: a note can show an image from anywhere, as markdown does.
# - connect-src: this origin (the API) and the provider hosts the browser
#   syncs with directly. A provider's hosts arrive with its adapter.
#   Dropbox: *.dropboxapi.com (api. for metadata, content. for bytes).
#   OneDrive: graph.microsoft.com for everything but file bytes, which come
#   from the item's pre-authenticated download URL on another host —
#   *.files.1drv.com or my.microsoftpersonalcontent.com for personal accounts,
#   *.sharepoint.com for work and school ones (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §5.2).
#   Google Drive: www.googleapis.com, the one host the adapter asks — file
#   bytes (`alt=media`) and uploads included (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §5.1). Whether
#   Google redirects a download elsewhere waits on the live check; don't add
#   *.googleusercontent.com ahead of it, as it takes in Apps Script web apps.
# Strict-Transport-Security: the shell holds the device's per-connection
# credentials in IndexedDB (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §6), so an origin an attacker can
# answer for once is an origin they can read them from. Two years, for this
# host alone. `includeSubDomains` and `preload` are deliberately absent: each
# is a commitment on behalf of whoever self-hosts this — served from an apex
# domain, the first forces HTTPS on every subdomain they own, for two years,
# and taking it back means every browser that saw it coming back over HTTPS
# to be told `max-age=0` — and it is theirs to make (docs/self-hosting.md).
# What it would protect is already kept to the origin: the credentials are in
# IndexedDB, and the flow cookie is `__Host-`, which no other host can set.
# A plain-HTTP local deployment is unaffected — browsers ignore the header off
# https, and these rules are Cloudflare's static-asset headers, which local dev
# does not serve through.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://*.dropboxapi.com https://graph.microsoft.com https://*.files.1drv.com https://my.microsoftpersonalcontent.com https://*.sharepoint.com https://www.googleapis.com; worker-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
